---
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { boundaries } from '../../data/boundaries';
import { CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';

const title = 'Boundaries | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'What the library does not do, what its examples assume, and how its two senses of quantum stay apart. Each row names the file that shows it.';
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/boundaries/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries</p>
    <h1>Where the library stops.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Each row below is a decision or a limit, and each names the file that shows it. The page
      describes the crate as it is, and it sets no schedule.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="limits">
    <h2>What does it not do?</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      {boundaries.map((b) => (
        <li class="row" id={b.id}>
          <p class={`status status-${b.status}`}>{b.mark}</p>
          <div>
            <h3>{b.title}</h3>
            <p class="t">{b.text}</p>
            <p class="ev">Shown in <code>{b.evidence}</code></p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="assumptions">
    <h2>What do the examples assume?</h2>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>The read-outs are inputs</dt>
        <dd>
          The crosstalk example states what each candidate predicts for each experiment, in
          <code>constants.rs</code>. The plan and the verdict follow from those numbers. The run
          shows the decision procedure reaching the right answer on stated assumptions, and it
          makes no claim about any device.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The observation is simulated</dt>
        <dd>
          The example draws its shots from the Born sampler at H1’s predicted read-out, then
          checks that the adjudication names H1. The example exits nonzero if it does not.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Cost is one arbitrary unit</dt>
        <dd>
          Tomography costs 200, a targeted intervention costs 1 and an echo costs 2, all in the
          same unit. The ratio comes from the example’s constants and is not a measurement of a
          laboratory.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The factors are diagonal</dt>
        <dd>
          Every candidate in the crosstalk example is a legal model by construction, so the Markov
          check has nothing to reject. The whole weight of the discrimination falls on the
          interventions.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One candidate is not a circuit</dt>
        <dd>
          The circuit version of the example keeps the shared bath as a factorization. A bath that
          drives two qubits needs a node with two output wires. Under the dilation’s leg
          convention that node has a leg of 256 dimensions, and the Markov union over its three
          legs is a 65536 × 65536 matrix with 2<sup>32</sup> entries, beyond the crate’s cap of
          2<sup>24</sup>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="modalities">
    <h2>How do the two senses of quantum stay apart?</h2>
    <p>
      The crate names two senses of quantum and keeps them apart by construction. The verifiable
      path is the default build and the target of the Lean proofs. The emergent path is a seam.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Modality</th><th>What it is</th><th>Build</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Modality"><b>Verifiable</b></td>
            <td data-label="What it is">Deterministic simulated Choi–Jamiołkowski operators, checked at the freeze boundary and backed by Lean proofs.</td>
            <td data-label="Build">Default</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Modality"><b>Emergent</b></td>
            <td data-label="What it is">A physical-QPU call lifted into the causal monad as a typed effect (<code>qpu_effect</code>), with an in-process <code>SimQpu</code> behind it.</td>
            <td data-label="Build"><code>qpu</code> feature</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The emergent seam is a typed boundary and not a driver. It adds no network or async
      dependency. Its source is under
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/src/types/qpu`}><code>src/types/qpu/</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-5) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h) + var(--space-4));
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .row { grid-template-columns: var(--w-label) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  .status { margin: 0; }
  .row h3 { font-size: var(--t-h4); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .t { color: var(--fg-1); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .ev { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin: 0; }
  .ev code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
</style>
